<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kharkiv Collegium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1726</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/collegium-russia">Collegium</a> was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> school in Kharkiv, founded in 1726 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Epifaniy Tikhorsky moved the Belgorod seminary there. Modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-mohyla-academy">Kyiv-Mohyla Academy</a>, it taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, rhetoric, philosophy and theology and was the leading school of Sloboda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in the 18th century; the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hryhorii-skovoroda">Hryhorii Skovoroda</a> taught there. Its role passed to Kharkiv University after 1805.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kharkiv-collegium.jpg" alt="Kharkiv Collegium" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: неизвестно / unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kharkov%27s_Collegium_XVIII-XIX.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
